        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> I really enjoy using your plugins. Even though I don't fully understand the code (yet) I appreciate that the whole plugin is <2k of code (with comments) and not a complex application ❤️\n\nThank you for kind words! I wouldn't recommend fully understanding the code for 'mini.completion', though. It has one of the most complex entangled code, mostly due to many moving parts involved (built-in completion, async execution, LSP spec, how different servers implement LSP spec, etc.).\n\n> Sidenote: The `mini.snippets` adds some strange characters after the completion is applied; they don't affect the text editing, but I'm letting you know just in case you know what's happening\n\nThis is expected visualization of empty tabstops. It is shown in [demo](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/readmes/mini-snippets.md#demo) and [documented](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/93cca0073778f4118c55a73a48b30d06ef064add/doc/mini-snippets.txt#L290-L291) in several places. It is useful exactly in the situation you show: without them it would be not obvious that the snippet has one regular and one final tabstop.\n\nTo adjust them (or remove by setting to empty string), [specify custom `config.expand.insert`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/93cca0073778f4118c55a73a48b30d06ef064add/doc/mini-snippets.txt#L708-L716).",
